Wait, `(node)->body` - but `Node` doesn't have a `body` field directly. Let me check the struct again:

```c
typedef struct _Node {
    union {
        struct {
            NodeType node_type;
            int status;
            struct _Node* parent;
            struct _Node* body;
        } base;
        StrNode       str;
        CClassNode    cclass;
        QuantNode     quant;
        ...
    } u;
} Node;
```

So `node->body` doesn't exist. But `NODE_QUANT_BODY` is used in `optimize_nodes` on line 6461:
```c
if (NODE_IS_ANYCHAR(NODE_QUANT_BODY(qn)))
```

Wait, `qn` is `QuantNode*` (from `QUANT_(node)`). And `QuantNode` has a `body` field directly. So `NODE_QUANT_BODY(qn)` accesses `qn->body` which is `((QuantNode*)node)->body`.

But wait, `NODE_QUANT_BODY` is defined as `((node)->body)`. If `node` is a `QuantNode*`, then `node->body` is the `body` field of `QuantNode`. But `QuantNode` has:
```c
typedef struct {
    NodeType node_type;
    int status;
    struct _Node* parent;
    struct _Node* body;
    ...
} QuantNode;
```

So `NODE_QUANT_BODY(qn)` where `qn = QUANT_(node)` accesses `qn->body`, which is the body of the quantifier node.

